by voluntarily engaging the CFA (and the NCPC, for that matter) in a “couple of staff-level

meetings.” Hr’g Tr. at 101:20–21 (Floca testimony); see also Opp. Br. at 17. The Court

disagrees. CFA (and NCPC) consultation processes entail far more than ad hoc, closed-door

staff meetings. At minimum, they require public input and actual, detailed submissions. See,

e.g., 45 C.F.R. § 2102.10 et seq. Whatever voluntary contact the Center has made with capital

planning agencies, it falls short of the purported legal requirements.




