omitted). And she may not manufacture standing by voicing approval for part of the very Board

decision that she claims injured her. See Nat’l Fam. Plan. & Reprod. Health Ass’n, Inc. v.

Gonzales, 468 F.3d 826, 831 (D.C. Cir. 2006) (“We have consistently held that self-inflicted harm

doesn’t satisfy the basic requirements for standing.”). There, to Defendants’ knowledge, is no

support in trust law, precedent, or history and tradition for Beatty’s suit in these circumstances,

and this Court should accordingly not grant relief.

