data/sources/coverage/kennedy-center/article_6951.txt:7:Instead, the judge ordered the Trump-aligned Kennedy Center to submit a sworn declaration from a knowledgeable representative of the center who can attest to the emergency repairs that necessitated the closure. Justice Department attorneys argued in a court filing Sept. 17 that the closure is "temporary" to address emergency repairs, not to begin a two-year renovation project Trump has pushed.
data/sources/coverage/kennedy-center/article_6951.txt:11:Without renovations, Trump told reporters on Sept. 16 that the Kennedy Center will "end up being ripped down" because of its dilapidated state. The same day, Trump was pictured through a window on Air Force One reading a poster that says, "Kennedy Center DEMOLISHED."
data/sources/coverage/kennedy-center/article_6951.txt:12:Beatty's attorneys cited both examples in a court filing as evidence that Trump is actively considering the demolition of the Kennedy Center. They asked the judge to clarify that his order issued in May ‒ which blocked a previous attempt to close the Kennedy Center ‒ also prohibits its demolition.
data/sources/kennedy-center/beatty-counsel-reaction-2026-09-17.txt:6:“We are grateful Judge Cooper is taking these escalating threats to the Kennedy Center seriously,” said Rep. Beatty’s counsel, Amb. Norm Eisen (ret.) of Democracy Defenders Action and Nathaniel Zelinsky of Washington Litigation Group. “Further defiance by the Trump administration would precipitate this into a full-blown constitutional crisis of the president’s own making. We are already on the precipice. Fortunately, the court and the nation are watching.”
