common-law trust principles, but also by extension, Congress’s chosen language. Still, it

remains unclear whether a trustee must be able to vote in order to participate meaningfully in the

trust’s affairs. For guidance, the Court turns back to the organic statute.

       Congress has delineated in granular detail whom the Board “shall be composed of.” See

20 U.S.C. § 76h(a)(2). It has permitted the Board to “function notwithstanding vacancies and

twelve members of the Board shall constitute a quorum for the transaction of business.” See id.

§ 76l(a). Furthermore, the Board is “authorized . . . to make such bylaws, rules, and regulations,

