such are non-voting or voting. And it has sometimes specified that non-voting members are to
participate in an “advisory capacity.” See, e.g., 15 U.S.C. § 657u(b)(2); 12 U.S.C. § 5321(b)(2).
But this variety of citations, none of which appear to deal with a trust instrumentality, is not
especially helpful in elucidating what Congress meant when it conferred the “usual powers and
obligations” on Kennedy Center trustees. The Court thus stands by its conclusion that the lack of
distinction between general and ex officio board members, coupled with the “usual powers and
obligations” language, places trustees on a default level playing field when it comes to voting.


